l documented repo, with some sensible workflows in place we will be able to determine next milestones to work on. The first mechanism that we need to establish is a prompt log system. I'm already used to some AI workflows from other proyects. One is jonatanbb, which you can find on ~/Descargas. I want you to go there and understand how the prompt logging is done, so that you may write up the relevant guide to replicate that prompt-logging method in CLAUDE.md. Note that there the repo is built around iterations and a living dex of source codes. None of that applies here. I just want you to log each turn (prompt plus your final answer) **VERBATIM**. If I just started a new session then you should start a new log on prompts. Otherwise just append to the current log file. Note that turns here should be separated by 2 blank lines, and that each contribution has to be labeled and dated. No need to put here the rule that the log files are only to be written into and not read. Note that I saved the first turn about the naming in the root of the repo just so that you could start this first log since that far back. You are to delete the md file after that, since that was its only purpose, to carry the first turn while the repo is not operative yet. That brings me to another philosophy paradigm I like to adhere to strictly, which is to minimice redundancy and clutter. The repo already has a versioning system in git that allows us to essentially go back in time through it, so there's absolutely no need to have documentation files becoming a log of themselves. What I mean by this is that, unless there's a real good reason for it, you should refrain to edit documents or code in a way that generates bloat. Maybe imprecise here, but what I mean is that for instance if we consciously changed some part of the algorithm, then the old version was not silently missed, so no need to explicitly say when documenting the new algorithm how things used to work previously. As a rule of thumb, 