The repo is set up — structure, workflows, and a first draft of the journal's opening section. Here is what it now looks like, and the choices I made along the way.

**What was built**

- `CLAUDE.md` — the workflow guide: the prompt-log system adapted from jonatanbb (`prompts/sessionNNNN.log` with the next unused 4-digit counter per session; each contribution labeled and dated; prompt and final answer verbatim; two blank lines between turns), the no-bloat rule ("documentation stays in the present"), and the three sections you asked to borrow: git in read mode only, system-wide software installs, and the whole code style section — including your em-dash convention, which I applied throughout the new docs.
- `prompts/session0000.log` — the claude.ai naming turn, carried over verbatim, after which I deleted `naming-session-01.md`. Since that was its own session it got its own log; this conversation lives in `session0001.log`, which already holds your prompt and this answer.
- `README.md` — I went with the option you were leaning toward: a short public face (what bubblink is, the two chained programs, status, layout, license) that points to the journal instead of embedding it. One argument beyond yours for this side: a README must stay free to serve the visitor — install instructions, usage examples, badges will eventually want in, and none of that belongs inside a narrative. The journal deserves a file that serves only the reader of ideas.
- `docs/approach.md` — the journal. A short front matter defines what the document is, then a draft of section 1: the motivation (a board too good to erase), the generalization (from stitching pixels to extracting the writing itself — placement, color, bubble skeleton — plus the re-inking half that turns it into a digital-asset tool), and the setup (the stable-state pipeline, the founding datasets, the milestone pacing). It is a draft for you to redline; when it reads "final" to you, milestone 1 is done.
- `milestones.md` — a small addition of my own, borrowed from jonatanbb's workflow since you explicitly plan to move milestone by milestone: milestone 1 stated, and the natural next fronts sketched. Per CLAUDE.md it is yours to maintain.

Two deliberate omissions: no `src/`, no `.gitignore`, no scaffolding — the no-bloat rule in CLAUDE.md covers structure too, so folders appear when something real needs them. And one wrinkle in that rule worth flagging: `docs/approach.md` is explicitly exempted, since its whole point is narrating the path — there, the past is content, not clutter.

**Remarks**

1. Your bubble construction is a known and beautiful object in disguise: the chain of maximal tangent disks approximates the *medial axis transform*, and your "distance to the first pixel of another color" is the *Euclidean distance transform* — for which exact linear-time algorithms exist (`scipy.ndimage` ships one). Good news twice: the expensive-sounding part is cheaper than feared, and there is solid theory to lean on when strokes branch (letters like `t` or `x` create branch points, which your local-maxima-on-the-boundary step will meet head on). I would start pure Python + NumPy and reach for C++ only after profiling says so — my bet is that cross-photo matching, not bubble-growing, ends up the hot spot.
2. Datasets: while memory is fresh, consider recording per set the facts the photos do not carry — pen colors used, shot order, approximate distance, board vs window. A one-line note per dataset folder suffices. Related: for future captures, use your phone's maximum JPEG quality (or RAW) if it allows — compression artifacts smear exactly the color boundaries that k-means will feed on.
3. Expect glare and lighting gradients to be one of the surprise difficulties: k-means on raw RGB may need a normalization step first. If it bites, it is worth a milestone of its own.
4. Nothing is committed — per the imported git policy, versioning is yours. The working tree holds everything above, ready for you to move things around and mark the breakpoint when it reads right.
